<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Molla Panah Vagif</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1717–<a href="/years/1797">1797</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> · Also known as Vagif</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Molla Panah Vagif was an Azerbaijani poet and statesman who served as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vizier">vizier</a> of the Karabakh Khanate under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ibrahim-khalil-khan">Ibrahim Khalil Khan</a>. His lyric poems, written in a simple style close to spoken Azerbaijani, made him one of the founders of modern Azerbaijani poetry. He was executed in 1797 during the turmoil that followed the assassination of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qajar-dynasty">Qajar</a> ruler Agha Mohammad Khan.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/molla-panah-vagif.jpg" alt="Molla Panah Vagif" width="320" height="227" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Post of Azerbaijan/Azermarka · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Stamps_of_Azerbaijan,_2014-1186.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>He was born in the village of Salahly in the Kazakh Sultanate. His parents, Mehdi agha and Aghqiz khanum, sent him to the local cleric Shafi Effendi to learn Arabic and Persian, and in 1759 the family moved to the Karabakh Khanate after fighting between the Kazakh Sultanate and the Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/georgia">Georgia</a>. He settled in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shusha">Shusha</a>, the capital of the khanate, and founded a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madrasa">madrasa</a> in its Saatli quarter.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>In 1769 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ibrahim-khalil-khan">Ibrahim Khalil Khan</a>, who had heard that he could predict lunar eclipses, invited him to court and, after he converted from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sunni-islam">Sunni Islam</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shiism">Shiism</a>, made him eshik aghasi, in charge of foreign affairs, an office he held for 27 years. He helped plan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shusha">Shusha</a>, visited <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tbilisi">Tbilisi</a> several times, where he befriended Prince Iulon of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/georgia">Georgia</a>, and began the khanate's correspondence with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catherine-the-great">Catherine II</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>. He tried without success to ally <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karabakh">Karabakh</a>, Georgia and the Erivan and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/talysh">Talysh</a> khanates against Qajar Iran.</p><p>He organised the defence of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shusha">Shusha</a> when Agha Mohammad Shah Qajar besieged it in 1795. According to the historian Mirza Adigozal bey, in a tale that may be apocryphal, the shah shot a taunting couplet of Urfi into the town, playing on the likeness of Shusha to the word for glass, and Vagif answered it with a couplet of his own. After 33 days the shah lifted the siege.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>When the shah returned in 1797, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shusha">Shusha</a> fell; Vagif was imprisoned, but the shah was assassinated that night. Muhammed bey <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/javanshir">Javanshir</a>, a nephew of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ibrahim-khalil-khan">Ibrahim Khalil Khan</a>, then had Vagif and his son Ali executed and his house plundered, and many of his verses were lost. His poems, first collected by Mirza Yusif Nersesov in 1856 and printed by Adolf Berge in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leipzig">Leipzig</a> in 1867, turned from the abstract religious <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theme-system">themes</a> of Sufi-leaning verse to everyday feelings in a language close to the vernacular, and many are sung by ashiks. His mausoleum in Shusha, built in the 1970s, was destroyed in 1992 during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-nagorno-karabakh-war">First Nagorno-Karabakh War</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Molla Panah Vagif” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Ibrahim Khalil Khan, from 1763, was a powerful ruler, and his vizier, the poet <b>Molla Panah Vagif</b>, shaped court culture.</blockquote><a href="/state/karabakh-khanate">Karabakh Khanate</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Yukhary Salahly, in what is now <a href="/countries/azerbaijan">Azerbaijan</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shusha">Shusha</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/azerbaijan">Azerbaijan</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=41.24,45.27&amp;year=1769" aria-label="Yukhary Salahly on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5656 693 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Molla Panah Vagif"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6106 963h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M6106 963h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=41.24,45.27&amp;year=1769">What was here in 1769? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
